The image shows several structures (the nitrophenol, aminophenol 46 and rifamycin S), so I chose the largest one, rifamycin S, which is the most complex molecule drawn. I wrote its SMILES from the drawing's connectivity, using stereocentre and double-bond assignments recalled from the known rifamycin skeleton rather than derived from the wedges in the image. I did not assign the quaternary acetal carbon, so that centre is undefined in the answer.

The structure has these parts:
- a naphthoquinone core carrying a free phenolic OH and an aromatic methyl;
- an amide NH linking the quinone to the ansa chain;
- the ansa chain: C(Me)=CH–CH=CH–CH(Me)–CH(OH)–CH(Me)–CH(OH)–CH(Me)–CH(OAc)–CH(Me)–CH(OMe)–CH=CH–O;
- the furanone ring, bridged through an oxygen to the quaternary carbon that carries a methyl.

I did not run a validity check on the SMILES.

SMILES: C[C@H]1/C=C/C=C(\C(=O)NC2=CC(=O)C3=C(C2=O)C4=C(C(=C3O)C)OC(C4=O)(O/C=C/[C@@H]([C@H]([C@H]([C@@H]([C@@H]([C@@H]([C@H]1O)C)O)C)OC(=O)C)C)OC)C)C

I read the structure in three parts and then joined them.

**Macrocycle (left).** It is a 15-membered diaza-crown ring containing 2 N, 3 O and 10 C. Going round the ring, the sequence is N–CH2CH2–O–CH2CH2–O–CH2CH2–N–CH2CH2–O–CH2CH2 back to the first N. One N carries a 2-methoxyphenyl group. The other N is bonded to the phthalide benzene ring.

**Phthalide (centre).** The benzene ring has these substituents in order:
- an OMe group;
- an N bonded to the crown ring, on the carbon next to the OMe carbon;
- a ring CH between the OMe carbon and the carbon bearing the spiro centre;
- a C=O of the lactone and the spiro carbon on adjacent ring carbons, joined through the lactone O.

**Xanthene (right).** The spiro carbon is the C9 position of a xanthene. Each xanthene benzo ring carries a 4-pyridyl group and an acetoxy group. Reading each ring from the spiro-bonded carbon, the order is CH, C(pyridyl), C(OAc), CH, C–O(ring). The ring oxygen bridges the two benzo rings.

No wedges or hashes are drawn, so I included no stereochemistry.

